From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 16:17:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02619/11362] [outer-loop] config rule-inducer step 2:
 interaction types MODELED (learnable vocabulary) but win-RULE is the hard
 Layer B

Modeled the config interaction types (operator direction, step 2). Found a small LEARNABLE mechanic
vocabulary by introspection:
  - progress_fill (lf52, vc33): keyboard advances a cursor filling cells (lf52 row-0 0->1). vc33 same
    type, ALREADY solves.
  - rule_rewrite_propagate (tr87): cycle a selected glyph -> propagates through a rule grid (13-29
    cells/press).
  - object_move (ka59): keyboard moves a ~19-cell block (move, not recolor).
  - click_toggle_indirect (sc25, tn36, s5i5): click a control toggles a target elsewhere.
  - large_region/one-time (dc22, bp35, g50t).

KEY FINDING: the interaction model is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT. Best-first search with each
mechanic's natural heuristic (progress-fill = maximize filled cells) does NOT solve lf52/dc22/g50t/
bp35 -- lf52's keyboard-reachable space is ~260 states and none win, because the win is NOT 'maximize
fill' but a SPECIFIC configuration (the win-RULE). The interaction model says WHAT actions do, not
WHICH config wins.

This cleanly SPLITS the rule-inducer into two layers: LAYER A (done) the interaction vocabulary --
small, learnable, introspectable; LAYER B (the remaining hard frontier) the WIN-RULE -- the relation
the config must satisfy, which the interaction model does not provide and generic heuristics do not
capture. Layer B is rule/relation synthesis -> the LLM-scene-reader (read the puzzle goal) VERIFIED by
the consistency energy (the project's propose-then-ground core). The path is now precisely two-layered.
results/arc3_config_interaction_models.json. Adversarial-verify: clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
